README-FLOATINGPOINT -- math.l, math881.l and fpu=================================================Microware's C compiler comes with two math libraries.  The runtimemodules that match them, math and math881, are in CMDS.    -l=math.l      software floating point.  Runs on any 68k.    -l=math881.l   uses a 68881/68882 coprocessor.  Needs one.They give the same answers.  math.l, measured against the true values:                        math.l              true value    sqrt(2)             1.4142135624        1.4142135624    exp(1)              2.7182818285        2.7182818285    sin(1)              0.8414709848        0.8414709848    pow(2,10)           1024.0000000000     1024    H(100)              5.1873775176        5.1873775176H(100) is the sum of 1/k for k = 1 to 100.Nearly everything on this disk that uses floating point was built withmath.l, so it runs with or without a coprocessor.  math881.l is fasteron a machine that has one.  paranoia and savage measure floating-pointaccuracy on your own system.Under os9exec, figures like exp(1) 2.7182831246, sin(1) 0.8414711041 orpow(2,10) 1024.0039 mean the emulator is a build from before 2026-09-14and needs updating.  A real 68k was never affected.fpu, for Ultra C----------------Ultra C generates coprocessor code whatever the machine.  `fpu' in CMDSis Microware's emulation module: with it installed, a machine with no68881/68882 runs that code.  It goes in your bootfile and your Initmodule's extension list, then you reboot.  DOC/fpu.doc is Microware'sinstructions and its distribution grant, which asks that the documentstay with the module.math.l and math881.l are for `cc' 3.2 and earlier, which built most ofthis collection.  To run what is here you need math and math881, notfpu.